    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a solid assessment. I'll look into the ESR values and VRM thermals during a stress test as you suggested. To be more specific, I'm running a custom kernel on this build for some specialized computations. If I find that the VRM instability is purely thermal-induced, would you recommend adjusting the undervolt offset to reduce the transient load, or is it safer to simply increase the TDP ceiling in the BIOS to maintain stability under sustained workloads? Also, are there any specific voltage ripple tolerances I should be aiming for to ensure long-term component longevity?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Adjusting the undervolt offset might indeed help reduce thermal stress on the VRM components. Undervolting can lead to lower dissipated power and consequently lower temperatures, thus reducing the stress on the VRM components. However, it is essential to do this carefully and incrementally, as undervolting too much can lead to system instability and unexpected behaviors, especially with a custom kernel. On the other hand, increasing the TDP ceiling allows the BIOS to operate at a higher specified thermal limit, which should not reduce VRM performance but can help avoid thermal throttling and system stability issues during prolonged high-load periods. The choice depends on the system's cooling efficiency and the extent of your workload.\n\nAs for voltage ripple tolerances, while they vary by motherboard model and manufacturer, it is generally recommended to have a voltage ripple of less than 200mV peak-to-peak for consumer-grade systems. For high-performance or workstation computers, especially those pushed to their limits regularly, aiming for a voltage ripple of less than 100mV peak-to-peak can help ensure component longevity and stability. This keeps the PWM (Pulse-Width Modulation) noise to a minimum and avoids excessive electrical stress on passive components like capacitors. If your BIOS allows",
      "source": "target",
